WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.405
A silkworm lives for about a month.

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:09.830
It eats nothing but the leaves
of the mulberry tree,

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.715
and in four weeks it grows some
ten thousand times heavier.

00:00:13.715 --> 00:00:19.027
Then, in three days, it wraps itself
in a single unbroken thread:

00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:22.460
a thousand metres of silk, sometimes more.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.995
Across Russian Turkestan, families raised
silkworms at home for centuries.

00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:32.738
A survey of the region published in nineteen
thirteen describes how the women who

00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:36.602
did the work carried the eggs in
little bags inside their clothes,

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.530
to hatch them with the warmth
of their own bodies.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.765
By nineteen forty,

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:46.870
the Uzbek Soviet republic was producing half of
all the silk cocoons in the Soviet Union.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.980
Behind that figure stood
millions of mulberry trees,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.850
and the people who had
learned how to grow them.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:58.031
In one family photograph, on an office
desk among papers and inkwells,

00:00:58.031 --> 00:01:00.770
stand two glass jars full of cocoons.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:06.141
The desk belonged to a man who began life as a
herd-boy in a village on the edge of the steppe.

00:01:19.141 --> 00:01:20.826
This is Ikan.

00:01:20.826 --> 00:01:26.732
The photograph was taken around eighteen
seventy-one, for the Turkestan Album:

00:01:26.732 --> 00:01:30.821
mud walls, flat roofs, a caravan
of camels passing by.

00:01:30.821 --> 00:01:33.285
Ikan was a farming village of Uzbeks,

00:01:33.285 --> 00:01:38.881
some twenty-five kilometres from the old city
of Turkestan, on the road to Tashkent.

00:01:38.881 --> 00:01:42.126
Turkestan itself was a
place of pilgrimage.

00:01:42.126 --> 00:01:47.405
Above its low houses rose the domes of the
mausoleum of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi,

00:01:47.405 --> 00:01:50.573
a Sufi poet and saint of
the twelfth century,

00:01:50.573 --> 00:01:53.811
whose shrine was built on
the orders of Timur.

00:01:53.811 --> 00:01:57.186
Ikan had a place of its own
in the history books.

00:01:57.186 --> 00:01:59.218
In December eighteen sixty-four,

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:04.743
a Kokand army and a company of Ural Cossacks
fought for three days outside the village,

00:02:04.743 --> 00:02:08.681
in one of the battles of the Russian
conquest of Central Asia.

00:02:08.681 --> 00:02:14.844
Gulam Babadjanov was born in Ikan in nineteen
oh-seven, into a family of dehqans,

00:02:14.844 --> 00:02:16.062
peasant farmers.

00:02:17.562 --> 00:02:23.147
What we know of his childhood comes from a short entry
in a book about the region's notable people.

00:02:23.147 --> 00:02:27.827
Entry number fifteen, printed beside his
portrait, gives the bare facts.

00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:31.692
His father died when Gulam
was one year old.

00:02:31.692 --> 00:02:35.904
His grandfather raised him until he
was eight, and then his mother,

00:02:35.904 --> 00:02:37.162
until he was eleven.

00:02:37.162 --> 00:02:40.631
From nineteen eighteen, the boy
worked for other people:

00:02:40.631 --> 00:02:44.782
he herded their animals and helped
with the sowing and the harvest.

00:02:44.782 --> 00:02:48.483
In the winter months, when there
was no work in the fields,

00:02:48.483 --> 00:02:50.742
he studied at a newly opened school.

00:02:50.742 --> 00:02:56.187
Those were years of revolution, civil
war and hunger in Turkestan.

00:02:56.187 --> 00:03:00.115
Schools of the new kind were only
just reaching the villages,

00:03:00.115 --> 00:03:03.851
and for a boy without a father,
one of them opened a door.

00:03:09.851 --> 00:03:14.318
In nineteen twenty-two, at the age of
fifteen, he set out for Tashkent,

00:03:14.318 --> 00:03:18.596
the capital of his own province and of
the whole Turkestan republic,

00:03:18.596 --> 00:03:21.176
some two hundred kilometres to the south.

00:03:21.176 --> 00:03:26.902
There he entered the preparatory class of the
Uzbek men's Institute of Education,

00:03:26.902 --> 00:03:28.316
known as the inpros.

00:03:28.316 --> 00:03:33.086
Despite its name, it was not a
university but a college for teachers.

00:03:33.086 --> 00:03:37.812
A Tashkent directory of nineteen
twenty-six lists it: six classes,

00:03:37.812 --> 00:03:42.611
two of them preparatory, at the corner
of Lenin and Engels streets.

00:03:42.611 --> 00:03:44.257
By official Soviet count,

00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:49.657
the state schools on Uzbek soil grew from a
hundred and sixty in nineteen thirteen

00:03:49.657 --> 00:03:52.818
to almost two thousand by
nineteen twenty-eight,

00:03:52.818 --> 00:03:55.321
and every one of them needed teachers.

00:03:55.321 --> 00:04:00.215
Gulam went through all six years, and
graduated in nineteen twenty-eight.

00:04:01.715 --> 00:04:05.245
While he studied, the map
around him was redrawn.

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:10.850
In nineteen twenty-four, Soviet Turkestan
was divided along national lines.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:15.490
Tashkent and Samarkand became part of
the new Uzbek Soviet republic;

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.607
Turkestan and Ikan were assigned to the
autonomous republic of the Kazakhs,

00:04:20.607 --> 00:04:22.040
within Soviet Russia.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:22.869
From then on,

00:04:22.869 --> 00:04:28.355
his home village and the city where he would
make his life lay in different republics.

00:04:28.355 --> 00:04:32.090
He was not the only young man from
Ikan to take this road.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.802
The poet Mirtemir, born there
three years after him,

00:04:35.802 --> 00:04:40.942
studied at the same institute in Tashkent
and then went on to Samarkand,

00:04:40.942 --> 00:04:41.870
as Gulam did.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.080
In nineteen twenty-eight, Samarkand
was the capital of Uzbekistan,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.055
and its new pedagogical institute was
drawing students from across the region.

00:04:51.055 --> 00:04:56.311
Exactly what Gulam studied there, the
documents found so far do not say.

00:05:02.311 --> 00:05:04.186
Silk depends on a tree.

00:05:04.186 --> 00:05:07.727
A silkworm will eat only the
leaves of the mulberry:

00:05:07.727 --> 00:05:12.700
in its short life a single worm eats
twenty to twenty-five grams of leaf,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.446
three quarters of it in its last
stage before it spins.

00:05:16.446 --> 00:05:20.811
A family's silk harvest was only as
good as the leaves it could pick.

00:05:20.811 --> 00:05:22.355
In the eighteen-sixties,

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:27.887
a disease of the silkworm called pébrine nearly
destroyed the silk industry of Europe,

00:05:27.887 --> 00:05:32.776
and Italian merchants travelled as far as
Bukhara in search of healthy eggs.

00:05:32.776 --> 00:05:34.717
By the early twentieth century,

00:05:34.717 --> 00:05:38.914
the old local breeds of Turkestan were
giving way to imported ones,

00:05:38.914 --> 00:05:43.236
and egg stations had begun to examine
the moths under the microscope.

00:05:43.236 --> 00:05:45.543
Silk was becoming a science.

00:05:47.043 --> 00:05:52.079
In nineteen twenty-seven, a research
institute for silk opened in Tashkent:

00:05:52.079 --> 00:05:55.368
the Central Asian Sericulture
Research Institute.

00:05:55.368 --> 00:05:58.892
Its mulberry specialist was
Professor Alexander Fedorov,

00:05:58.892 --> 00:06:02.227
the author of books on the
mulberry and its breeding,

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:07.198
who tested Japanese varieties of the tree on
the institute's experimental farm.

00:06:07.198 --> 00:06:10.958
In the family's memory, Fedorov
was Gulam's teacher.

00:06:10.958 --> 00:06:13.323
The tree became Gulam's subject.

00:06:14.823 --> 00:06:18.718
By the middle of the nineteen-thirties
he was writing about it himself.

00:06:18.718 --> 00:06:24.062
The national bibliography of Soviet
Uzbekistan lists two booklets under his name,

00:06:24.062 --> 00:06:24.985
both in Uzbek,

00:06:24.985 --> 00:06:29.340
printed in the Latin alphabet that the
language used from the late

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.913
nineteen-twenties until nineteen forty.

00:06:31.913 --> 00:06:36.635
The first, in nineteen thirty-four, was
on growing mulberry saplings,

00:06:36.635 --> 00:06:41.563
issued in five thousand copies by the
Komsomol's farm technical station.

00:06:41.563 --> 00:06:43.958
The second, in nineteen thirty-five,

00:06:43.958 --> 00:06:48.949
was a handbook of thirty-five pages on
growing tall-stemmed mulberry trees,

00:06:48.949 --> 00:06:51.011
written for collective farmers,

00:06:51.011 --> 00:06:55.070
for brigade leaders on state farms
and in mulberry nurseries,

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.533
and for the managers of silk-growing.

00:06:57.533 --> 00:07:01.112
They were practical books, for
people who worked the land.

00:07:02.612 --> 00:07:05.792
The republic was planting
mulberries by the million.

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:09.548
A decree of nineteen forty,
signed by Molotov and Stalin,

00:07:09.548 --> 00:07:12.975
counted thirty-seven million
trees, set new targets,

00:07:12.975 --> 00:07:18.907
and noted that some two thousand incubators had
replaced the old way of hatching the eggs.

00:07:18.907 --> 00:07:20.043
A few months later,

00:07:20.043 --> 00:07:25.487
cutting down a mulberry tree without the
government's permission became a criminal offence,

00:07:25.487 --> 00:07:29.077
and for every tree felled, ten
young ones had to be planted.

00:07:29.077 --> 00:07:31.451
Silk had become a matter of state.

00:07:37.451 --> 00:07:39.871
Then the family's story breaks.

00:07:39.871 --> 00:07:43.733
In nineteen thirty-eight, at the
height of the Great Terror,

00:07:43.733 --> 00:07:47.466
an anonymous letter was enough to
end a career, or a life.

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:53.523
His family remembers that Gulam Babadjanov was
arrested that year on a false denunciation,

00:07:53.523 --> 00:07:55.811
and spent eleven months in prison.

00:07:55.811 --> 00:07:56.752
In Uzbekistan,

00:07:56.752 --> 00:08:02.129
the terror of nineteen thirty-seven and
thirty-eight swept away a large share of

00:08:02.129 --> 00:08:04.818
the republic's managers and specialists,

00:08:04.818 --> 00:08:06.431
and thousands were shot.

00:08:06.431 --> 00:08:08.417
Silk science was not spared:

00:08:08.417 --> 00:08:14.091
one of Tashkent's leading silk professors had
already spent two years in prison,

00:08:14.091 --> 00:08:16.786
on a false charge of spying for Japan.

00:08:16.786 --> 00:08:21.509
In November nineteen thirty-eight, Moscow
ordered the mass arrests to stop,

00:08:21.509 --> 00:08:27.681
and in the following year tens of thousands of people
were released when their cases were dropped.

00:08:27.681 --> 00:08:33.227
The poet Mirtemir, from Ikan, had himself
been arrested in nineteen thirty-two,

00:08:33.227 --> 00:08:34.491
and soon released.

00:08:34.491 --> 00:08:40.218
A fair and careful judge, the family says,
dismissed the charges against Gulam,

00:08:40.218 --> 00:08:41.451
and he came home.

00:08:41.451 --> 00:08:46.192
His name is not in the Memorial or Open
List databases of the repressed;

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:49.418
someone released without a
sentence often is not.

00:08:50.918 --> 00:08:55.068
In nineteen thirty-nine, he
completed his postgraduate studies;

00:08:55.068 --> 00:09:00.601
he went on to defend a dissertation and become
a Candidate of Agricultural Sciences,

00:09:00.601 --> 00:09:01.853
and later a docent.

00:09:01.853 --> 00:09:05.364
His field remained
sericulture, and the mulberry.

00:09:11.664 --> 00:09:15.844
In June nineteen forty-one, war
reached the Soviet Union.

00:09:15.844 --> 00:09:20.356
Factories, institutes and people were
evacuated to Central Asia;

00:09:20.356 --> 00:09:25.784
Moscow's Timiryazev Agricultural Academy
itself spent two years in Samarkand.

00:09:25.784 --> 00:09:28.722
A parachute factory from
Tushino, near Moscow,

00:09:28.722 --> 00:09:31.596
worked in Tashkent until
nineteen forty-four,

00:09:31.596 --> 00:09:36.387
and Soviet-era figures credit Uzbekistan
with some three hundred and thirty

00:09:36.387 --> 00:09:38.559
thousand parachutes for the front.

00:09:38.559 --> 00:09:39.731
Silk was scarce:

00:09:39.731 --> 00:09:46.254
the army's standard troop parachute of nineteen
forty-one had a canopy of cotton percale.

00:09:46.254 --> 00:09:51.182
Soviet histories say that Uzbekistan's silk
mills wove cloth for parachutes,

00:09:51.182 --> 00:09:56.499
and by their count the republic delivered
fifty-four thousand tonnes of cocoons to

00:09:56.499 --> 00:09:58.704
the state over the four war years.

00:09:58.704 --> 00:10:03.814
Silk was a strategic material, and the people
who knew how to grow it were needed.

00:10:03.814 --> 00:10:06.139
In the autumn of nineteen forty-one,

00:10:06.139 --> 00:10:11.564
the Tashkent Agricultural Institute ran crash
courses for district silk agronomists.

00:10:11.564 --> 00:10:16.863
His family remembers Gulam's war as work on
silk for the front, for parachutes;

00:10:16.863 --> 00:10:22.498
the state later awarded him the medal For
Valiant Labour in the Great Patriotic War.

00:10:28.498 --> 00:10:32.889
The institute he would lead had been
founded on the twenty-sixth of May,

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:33.864
nineteen thirty,

00:10:33.864 --> 00:10:39.658
when the agricultural faculty of the Central Asian
State University became a school of its own.

00:10:39.658 --> 00:10:44.478
In nineteen thirty-four it took the
name it would keep for decades:

00:10:44.478 --> 00:10:47.068
the Tashkent Agricultural Institute.

00:10:47.068 --> 00:10:50.209
Part of it had grown out of a
sericulture institute,

00:10:50.209 --> 00:10:52.928
and silk was taught there
from the beginning.

00:10:52.928 --> 00:10:54.129
In nineteen forty,

00:10:54.129 --> 00:10:59.132
the same decree on silk ordered its
sericulture faculty to be strengthened,

00:10:59.132 --> 00:11:04.469
to train at least seventy-five silk
agronomists a year from nineteen forty-four.

00:11:05.969 --> 00:11:10.112
At the institute he rose step by
step: head of a department,

00:11:10.112 --> 00:11:15.361
then deputy director for research and
teaching, and from nineteen fifty-two,

00:11:15.361 --> 00:11:16.259
its director.

00:11:16.259 --> 00:11:18.944
The family's photographs show him at work.

00:11:18.944 --> 00:11:23.104
He stands at the telephone in his
office, under a painted frieze.

00:11:23.104 --> 00:11:28.298
He sits at his desk, with a poster of the
plant breeder Ivan Michurin on the wall,

00:11:28.298 --> 00:11:32.479
the official emblem of Soviet
agricultural science in those years.

00:11:32.479 --> 00:11:37.574
He sits over papers with two colleagues, the
two jars of cocoons between them.

00:11:37.574 --> 00:11:41.644
In the laboratory, he stands among
students at their microscopes.

00:11:41.644 --> 00:11:46.360
Several of them still wear army tunics:
young men back from the front,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.179
studying to be agronomists.

00:11:48.179 --> 00:11:53.198
From nineteen forty-seven, the institute
published its own scientific proceedings.

00:11:54.698 --> 00:11:57.278
He took students out into
the fields as well.

00:11:57.278 --> 00:12:02.164
In the photographs he walks among young
trees, examines a budding shoot,

00:12:02.164 --> 00:12:05.218
and holds up a tall sapling
for two students.

00:12:05.218 --> 00:12:08.369
In a parade photograph from
about nineteen fifty,

00:12:08.369 --> 00:12:11.713
the institute's column marches
under its own banner.

00:12:11.713 --> 00:12:12.871
By official figures,

00:12:12.871 --> 00:12:16.575
the republic still raised about half
of all Soviet silk cocoons:

00:12:16.575 --> 00:12:20.048
in nineteen fifty-three, more than
thirteen thousand tonnes.

00:12:20.048 --> 00:12:24.661
He sat on the academic council of the
sericulture research institute,

00:12:24.661 --> 00:12:29.609
was elected to the Tashkent city soviet
and to the city's Party committee,

00:12:29.609 --> 00:12:36.228
and was deputy chairman of the republic's society for
spreading political and scientific knowledge.

00:12:36.228 --> 00:12:42.358
For his work he received the Order of the Red Banner of
Labour and the Order of the Badge of Honour.

00:12:42.358 --> 00:12:45.609
He had come to this desk from a
herd-boy's childhood,

00:12:45.609 --> 00:12:49.413
by way of a teachers' college and
the science of the mulberry.

00:12:49.413 --> 00:12:53.787
He had learned his science from the ground
up, and he taught it the same way.

00:12:59.787 --> 00:13:02.777
Gulam Babadjanov died on the twentieth
of May, nineteen fifty-five.

00:13:02.777 --> 00:13:06.275
The obituaries speak of a short illness;

00:13:06.275 --> 00:13:10.297
his family remembers an
operation that failed.

00:13:10.297 --> 00:13:12.482
He was still in his forties.

00:13:12.482 --> 00:13:17.359
Two days later, the republic's
Russian-language newspaper, Pravda Vostoka,

00:13:17.359 --> 00:13:21.247
and the Uzbek-language Tashkent
daily printed his obituary,

00:13:21.247 --> 00:13:23.224
signed by a group of comrades,

00:13:23.224 --> 00:13:28.101
with notices of mourning from the city's
Party committee, the city soviet,

00:13:28.101 --> 00:13:32.517
the Ministry of Agriculture and the
sericulture research institute.

00:13:32.517 --> 00:13:38.056
Six days after his death came the twenty-fifth
anniversary of the institute's founding.

00:13:40.856 --> 00:13:46.317
He left a widow, Habiba Mirsagatova, a
schoolteacher, and seven daughters,

00:13:46.317 --> 00:13:48.826
the youngest still small children.

00:13:48.826 --> 00:13:53.916
Habiba raised them, and the eldest,
Elmira, helped her bring up the others.

00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:56.891
All seven went on to higher education.

00:13:56.891 --> 00:14:02.064
Among them were an endocrinologist, an
ophthalmologist and a cardiologist;

00:14:02.064 --> 00:14:07.236
a philologist; a chemical engineer; and
teachers of music and of language.

00:14:07.236 --> 00:14:09.842
Two of them earned doctoral degrees.

00:14:11.342 --> 00:14:16.578
Little has been found in print so far: two
small handbooks on mulberry trees,

00:14:16.578 --> 00:14:22.017
a few lines in a book of the region's people,
and an obituary in two newspapers.

00:14:22.017 --> 00:14:27.674
The rest survives in photographs: an
office, a laboratory, a field,

00:14:27.674 --> 00:14:29.447
a banner in a parade.

00:14:29.447 --> 00:14:32.232
And it survives in the people he taught.

00:14:32.232 --> 00:14:34.511
Each spring, in the silk villages,

00:14:34.511 --> 00:14:39.203
the mulberry trees are stripped of their
leaves to feed the silkworms;

00:14:39.203 --> 00:14:41.482
and each year they grow them back.

00:14:41.482 --> 00:14:45.950
Gulam Babadjanov's life began in a
village on the edge of the steppe,

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:51.131
and ran through the classrooms and
laboratories of Tashkent, to seven daughters,

00:14:51.131 --> 00:14:53.138
and their children, and theirs.

